{
  "doc_id": "MIMIC_IV_NOTE:12915526-DS-16:discharge_sections",
  "register": "notes",
  "source_dataset": "MIMIC_IV_NOTE",
  "text": "Allergies:\ncodeine / Demerol\n\nChief Complaint:\nConfusion, low blood sugar\n\nHistory of Present Illness:\n___ is a ___ year old man with a history of chronic \npancreatitis, IDDM, Chronic opiod use who was brought into the \nhospital by ambulance after being found at home confused and \nhypoglycemic on well check at residence. \n\n___ is confused, disoriented and inattentive, he is unable to \nprovide much history aside from indicating he was confused, does \nnot remember anything before coming to the ED and does not know \nhow he got here. His PCP is unable to be reached for collateral \ninformation, he has a sister who lives in ___ but has not \nbeen involved recently. His home caretaker who found patient is \nunknown to this provider nor is there a phone number to reach \nthat individual. I called the mainline to his known ___ \n(___) and left a message with my cell #, \nas of writing this note I have not heard back from home \nservices. History is obtained via collateral information from \nthe ED and attempted outreach to ___ service and PCP ___. \n    \nPer the ED report the patient was found at home slurring his \nspeech and there were multiple open bottles of pills sprawled \nout on his floor. His FSBG was 50 and came up to 134 with oral \nglucose. Of note, he presented to the ED with a similar \npresentation two weeks prior for hypoglycemia without clear \netiology. \n\nIn the ED, initial vitals were 99.7 78 152/65 16 93% RA. Labs \nwere notable for severe respiratory acidosis, and a urine \ntoxicology positive for methadone and benzos.     \n \nOn the floor, he is confused, disoriented, inattentive, unable \nto provide much history nor is he able to recall his home \nmedications, specifically citing several different doses of \ninsulin. \n \nReview of systems:  \n(+) Per HPI  \n(-) Denies fever, chills, night sweats, recent weight loss or \ngain. Denies headache, sinus tenderness, rhinorrhea or \ncongestion. Denies cough, shortness of breath. Denies chest pain \nor tightness, palpitations. Denies nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, \nconstipation or abdominal pain. No recent change in bowel or \nbladder habits. No dysuria. Denies arthralgias or myalgias. \nOtherwise ROS is negative.\n\nBrief Hospital Course:\n___ yo man with history of insulin dependent diabetes mellitus, \nchronic pancreatitis who presents with confusion and \nhypoglycemia, presume medication related from inappropriate \ndosing.  It appears that he was struggling to manage safely at \nhome after having lived in a nursing home for the past ___ \nyears.  There is clear evidence of medication mismanagement and \nconcern for falls at home.  \n \n# Hypoglycemia\n# Insulin dependent Diabetes Mellitus, Tpe II\nHypoglycemia is likely iatrogenic from inappropriate insulin \ndosing. He presented confused, was found with empty pill bottles \non the floor and was unable to provide this provider with \naccurate insulin doses. Furthermore, in discussion with his \nsister there are significant concerns about him \nself-administering insulin and he has had trouble managing \ninsulin for many years with many prior admissions for \nhypogylcemia. \n\nHe was seen by the ___ consult service and his insulin \nregimen was changed to ___ insulin BID dosing without any \nfurther hypoglycemia.   Sliding scale should also be continued \nand patient should f/u at ___. \n \n# Encephalopathy, ? underlying dementia\nToxic-metabolic likely multifactorial from hyopogylcemia and \npotentially related to polypharmacy with benzos and methadone, \nboth positive in urine and he may also extra dosed these as \nwell. No clear infectious source and he denied symptoms, he has \nno asterixis and no prior history to suggest hepatic \nencephalopathy. \n\nHe had significant difficulty telling me details of his life at \nhome, and kept asking about where his mother was (although \nmother is deceased as of ___ years ago).  His sister describes \nincreasing memory impairment at home recently.  Head CT shows \nold lacunar infarct and chronic small vessel disease.  He needs \nformal evaluation by outpatient cognitive neurology.  \nPresentation is concerning for underlying dementia with \nsuperimposed delirium due to polypharmacy and recurrent \nhypoglycemia at home.  Patient does not have signs of \n___ Disease on exam.  \n\n# Gait \nHe is ataxic when ambulating; neurologic exam also suggestive of \ncerebellar dysfunction.  Consideration can be given to \noutpatient MRI.  B12 normal.  Gait is not Parkinsonian.  He \nshould also f/u with outpatient neurology.  He did not want to \nhave an MRI while hospitalized.  \n \n# Acute renal failure\nResolved with IVFs\n  \n# Acute respiratory acidosis\nBased on ABG in the ED, his respiratory status appears normal, \nlungs clear, moving air well and not hypoventilating on \nadmission examination. Resolved spontaneously ithout \nintervention.\n\n# Chronic pancreatitis - partial pancreatectomy per patient \nreport\n# Anxiety\n# Chronic opiate use\nPatient had been on Xanax and this was discontinued; he had not \nagitation or anxiety symptoms while hospitalized and this \nmedication should not be restarted.  \nHis methadone was continued in the hospital; would encourage \nrehab to speak to prescribing provider to discuss taper of \nmedication. Patient is on methadone for right chronic shoulder \npain.  Of note, he also has a history of opiate abuse in the \npast.  He has full ROM with his right shoulder and did not \ncomplain of shoulder pain while hospitalized.\n\nIn regards to diagnosis of chronic pancreatitis, last CT scan at \n___ in ___ showed calcifications in pancreas consistent with \nchronic pancreatitis, but patient without diarrhea (c/w \nmalabsorption from chronic pancreatitis) nor abdominal pain.  \nHis creon was continued for now, but the indication is somewhat \nunclear.  \n\nHe was continued on bupropion, which is presumably for anxiety; \npatient did not endorse anxiety symptoms while hospitalized.  \nWould also encourage rehab to speak to PCP about need for this \nmedication, consideration of taper.  \n\n# Hypertension:  Numerous BPs 150-160s; started low dose \namlodipine.  Decided against starting aCE inhitibor given his \nmild hyperkalemia (K often 5) \n  \n# Placement\nWill be discharged to rehab; given prolonged delirium, ? \nunderlying dementia, medication complexity, gait disturbance it \nappears unlikely that he will be able to return home safely at \nthis point.  \n   \nTransitional Issues:\n- Contact: ___ ___ ___ is his HCP and \nsister and knows him well.  I spoke to her at length on the day \nof discharge.  I encouraged ___ to transfer his outpatient \ncare to ___ as he gets hospitalized at ___ but all of his \nproviders are at ___, making his care somewhat \ndisjointed.\n\nDischarge Diagnosis:\nActive:\n- Hypogylcemia\n- Insulin Dependent Diabetes Mellitus Type II\n- Delirium\n-? Dementia\n- Ataxia",
